src/js/shvr/ holds the SHVR reader, ported from keycard-tech/shell-units web/ at tag 20261112 (that
project's own code, MIT), in these files and with these changes:

  errors.ts   web/errors.js        — no change but types and this header
  cbor.ts     web/cbor.js          — no change but types and this header
  ur.ts       web/ur.js            — imports bytewords from @blockchaincommons/uniform-resources/bytewords
                                     instead of their vendor bundle, and folds the body to lower case
                                     before decoding, which is what their bundle's decoder does
  db.ts       web/db.js            — imports secp256k1, sha256 and the byte helpers from @noble/*
                                     instead of their vendor bundle; drops an unused constant; drops
                                     a prehash option that noble 2.4's recoverPublicKey does not take

Relative imports end in .ts rather than .js, because Node runs the tests by stripping types and
resolves a specifier exactly as written.

Their web/dist/*.js vendor bundles are not used here: Parcel builds the same packages directly.

  @noble/curves                        2.4.0        MIT
  @noble/hashes                        2.4.0        MIT
  @blockchaincommons/uniform-resources 1.0.0-beta.5 BSD-2-Clause-Patent  (bytewords, from the authors of
                                                                        the BCR-2020-012 spec the
                                                                        firmware implements)
  html5-qrcode                         2.3.8        MIT        (the page's camera, not this reader's)
  qrious                               4.0.2        MIT        (the page's QR canvas, same)

Upstream's page takes bytewords from @qntx/ur. This site does not: a package with one maintainer and a
handful of downloads a month is not evidence that its table and checksum were checked, and bytewords
sits between the device and the signature. The substitution is safe because the wire format is pinned
by test/verify.test.ts against things other than itself — CRC-32 re-derived with node:zlib, the framing
rules the device applies, and the 256-word table read out of upstream's own vendor bundle at 20261112,
whose words and minimal spellings these are identical to.

docs/format.md in shell-units is the spec this code implements. test/verify.test.ts is ported from
that repo's tests/verify.test.mjs against these fixtures, so a divergence between this port and
upstream shows up as a failing test rather than as a difference nobody noticed.
